# Data licensing — Beelzebub's Tales

This file applies to the extracted text in `data/` and the source PDF
`Beelzebubs_Tales.pdf`. It does **not** apply to the scripts or schema in this
repository, which are MIT-licensed (see `LICENSE`).

## The underlying text

*Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson* (All and Everything, First Series) was
written by **G. I. Gurdjieff** (1866–1949) and first published in 1950 by
Routledge & Kegan Paul.

Gurdjieff died on 29 October 1949. Under the life-of-author-plus-70-years
copyright regime (EU, UK, and most Berne-Convention signatories), his works
entered the public domain on **1 January 2020**. The English text of
*Beelzebub's Tales* is therefore in the public domain in those jurisdictions.

In the United States, public-domain status for non-US-author works is more
complex; this work appears to have entered US public domain through normal
expiry of copyright on works first published outside the US under the rules
applicable to its publication date.

## The typesetting / edition

The source PDF (`Beelzebubs_Tales.pdf`, MD5/SHA-256 in `MANIFEST.json`) was
produced in 2019 by *joshuatilton* using the Vellum publishing tool (visible
in the PDF metadata). It is a re-typesetting of the original English text.

The text content of that edition is the underlying public-domain text. The
specific page numbering, paragraph anchors (`1.4`, `1.5-6`, ...), and visual
design of that edition are the original work of the typesetter. The
**anchors** are reused in this repository as a citation system: they are short
factual references to page numbers in a published edition, which is a
non-substantial part of the typesetter's creative contribution.

## What this repository republishes

- The full English text, in plain JSON, with paragraph-level anchors that
  refer back to the page numbering of the joshuatilton 2019 edition.
- Per-chapter structuring (chapter number + title) that matches the original
  Gurdjieff manuscript and all subsequent editions.
- The source PDF itself, retained verbatim for reproducibility.

If you redistribute the JSON data downstream, please include a citation to
either the original Gurdjieff work or this repository (or both):

> *Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson*, by G. I. Gurdjieff (1950). Text in the
> public domain. Machine-readable JSON edition via
> `github.com/sajad2025/beelzebub`, anchored to the joshuatilton/Vellum 2019
> typesetting.

## A note on Gurdjieff's intent

Gurdjieff's preface to this book ("Friendly Advice") explicitly invites
readers to read each passage three times, the third time trying to "fathom
the gist." This repository tries to honor that intent by preserving paragraph
boundaries and the printed anchors exactly, so any future reader app can
present the book as it was meant to be read rather than as a flat blob of
text.
